From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] setupapi: Retry a \\?\ inf name as given when the normalized one fails.

SetupOpenInfFileW() runs every name with a path separator through
GetFullPathNameW() before opening it. GetFullPathNameW() normalizes
\\?\ names too and, as on Windows, drops a trailing dot from a
directory name, while CreateFileW() would have used the \\?\ name
literally. wineboot opens wine.inf through such a name built from
WINEDATADIR, so with the runtime under a directory such as /Users/name./
the inf is not found, the prefix gets no system files and the next
process fails with c0000135.

Reproduced on this base with 0171 applied: the runtime-under-a-dotted-
directory case still exits 53, and the trace shows RtlGetFullPathName_U()
on the \\?\ name followed by CreateFileW() on the name without the dot.

The normalized name is still tried first, so every name that opens today
opens the same file in the same way. Only when that fails, the name
starts with \\?\ and normalizing changed it, the name is opened as
given, and that name is kept as the file name.

--- a/dlls/setupapi/parser.c
+++ b/dlls/setupapi/parser.c
@@ -1106,6 +1106,23 @@
         }
         GetFullPathNameW( name, len, path, NULL );
         handle = CreateFileW( path, GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, 0, 0 );
+        /* A \\?\ name is literal, but GetFullPathNameW() drops a trailing dot
+         * from a directory name, as on Windows. Retry the name as given. */
+        if (handle == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE && !wcsncmp( name, L"\\\\?\\", 4 ) && wcscmp( name, path ))
+        {
+            WCHAR *literal = wcsdup( name );
+
+            if (literal)
+            {
+                handle = CreateFileW( literal, GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, 0, 0 );
+                if (handle != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
+                {
+                    free( path );
+                    path = literal;
+                }
+                else free( literal );
+            }
+        }
     }
     else  /* try Windows directory */
     {
